        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> @echasnovski I think you should also make little UI changes. I've experienced that as soon as you make changes to a file then the currently highlighted tab(buffer) becomes dim & then looks similar to color of other tabs while something like bufferline plugin shows a green filled circle.\r\n> \r\n> I've seen this behavior with multiple themes.\r\n\r\nThe reason you see this is because by default all highlight groups are linked to Neovim's builtin highlight groups in order to ensure that they are somehow aligned with your color scheme. in 'mini.tabline' they are linked [like this](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/6cb7cdb1cd7f111784d5d971fa41a655e11df4b3/lua/mini/tabline.lua#L102-L110), which seem like a reasonable choice to make a cohesive set of highlights. In ideal world, all colorschemes out there would overwrite these highlight groups to make them more appropriate to its visual style. Unfortunately, this is not the case.\r\n\r\nAt the moment, you have the following options:\r\n- Manually customize highlight groups you don't like.\r\n- Use 'mini.base16' with palette of your choice. It supports all 'mini.nvim' highlight groups.\r\n- Propose changes to a colorscheme of your choice through a PR or wait for me to do it for most popular colorschemes out there (it is next on my TODO list).",
            "created_at": "2022-02-08T08:58:12Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/19#issuecomment-1032365119",
